+# Webhook / Admission Fail‑Open vs Fail‑Closed Runbook
+
+Purpose
+- Document operational steps and decision criteria for switching admission/webhook behavior when the image/model provenance webhook degrades.
+
+Key env toggles
+- FAIL_ON_ERROR=false -> fail‑open (allow when cosign/Rekor checks error)
+- FAIL_ON_ERROR=true -> fail‑closed (deny when checks error)
+
+Detection
+- Alert if webhook readiness < 1 replica, or > X% requests return 5xx, or cosign latency spikes.
+
+Fail‑open workflow (low-assurance mode)
+1. Set FAIL_ON_ERROR=false in webhook deployment:
+   kubectl set env deploy/image-signature-webhook -n kube-system FAIL_ON_ERROR=false
+2. Scale webhook to healthy pod count and add temporary rule exceptions in Gatekeeper if needed.
+3. Post incident: collect cosign/logs, reconcile missing Rekor entries, run replay checks for images that were allowed during outage.
+
+Fail‑closed workflow (strict mode)
+1. Set FAIL_ON_ERROR=true ONLY after webhook service reliability is restored.
+2. Run a short audit window in staging before flipping prod.
+
+Fallback (if webhook cannot be restored)
+1. Consider disabling ValidatingWebhookConfiguration temporarily to avoid blocking critical deployments:
+   kubectl delete validatingwebhookconfiguration image-signature-webhook-config
+2. Convene security/ops and require manual validation for image promotions during outage.
+
+Postmortem
+- Root cause analysis, Rekor availability checks, cosign runtime errors and any unsigned artifacts allowed during fail-open must be listed and mitigated.
+
